WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:05.070
Hello and welcome to the second part of our exercise.

2
00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.949
This is where we're actually going to be spawning some meshes into the world

3
00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:13.550
so that we can start off with the creation of our forest.

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:20.800
This is where we left off. We have spawned a bunch of points by sampling the landscape

5
00:00:21.519 --> 00:00:27.199
and all we have is two nodes at this point because we get landscape data

6
00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:33.359
and the surface sampler. So let's have a look at the surface sampler.

7
00:00:33.359 --> 00:00:40.079
Now you can see in the top right corner here we have a number of options available to us.

8
00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:46.880
The main ones that are of interest are probably the points per squared meter. So this is going

9
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.240
to establish in each square meter how many points we're going to generate. At the moment

10
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:01.439
this is set to 0.1. I think we're going to leave it there, but you'll see here if I was to come in

11
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:15.700
and reduce the value to say 0.02 and we were to look at the world we would get even less here.

12
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.060
So if we increase the value we're going to have more and if we decrease the value it's

13
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:25.219
going to go down. The points extent this is going to show you how big our point mesh is

14
00:01:25.219 --> 00:01:31.780
here. We're using the PCG cube but this can be swapped over. We could use a completely different

15
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:38.420
mesh and it would look completely different. I'd advise you stick with the cube really.

16
00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:47.170
But it's going to show you the extent so this is going to change the size of them.

17
00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:53.969
If I was to put in a thousand on the height here and check it out you're going to see we

18
00:01:53.969 --> 00:01:59.650
could have a whole load of sticking up points and this will become more relevant as we're going

19
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:09.340
through. I'm going to reset that for the time being and let's actually do something with these

20
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:18.020
nodes. Turn this off. I'm going to drag out and I'm going to add in a static mesh spawner.

21
00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:25.139
Now this node is going to allow us to actually spawn some meshes into the world

22
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:31.780
and with it selected in the details panel there is a couple of things that are worth us looking at.

23
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:39.379
Now the first one that I want to really look at is the mesh entries. You can see this is

24
00:02:39.379 --> 00:02:47.919
an array under the mesh selector. I'm going to add in two entries here and this is going to

25
00:02:47.919 --> 00:02:54.240
allow us to spawn or at least have a selection of two different meshes that it can select from.

26
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:59.229
Now you can open this up manually and under the descriptor you can find that we have the

27
00:02:59.229 --> 00:03:07.550
static meshes here. What I'm going to do though is I am going to search at the top for static

28
00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:15.620
mesh. The nice thing about this is it's just going to show me the static meshes so I can

29
00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:19.860
manually select it without having to sort through all these drop-down boxes.

30
00:03:20.979 --> 00:03:34.590
So for the first one I'm going to search for my bush and the second one I'm going to search for

31
00:03:37.229 --> 00:03:43.629
my tree. Let's see, here you go, my tree. Okay, so we are selecting two meshes.

32
00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:47.310
Now let's have a quick look at some of the other options here.

33
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.400
Now there are a lot of different options of how we are creating these and a couple of them

34
00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:04.400
that are useful is for example we have the mobility ray tracing. We've also got the collision

35
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.960
setting. Now for the tree, for the bush I don't mind if we walk through it at this point,

36
00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:17.839
however for the tree I would quite like the collision preset to block it. So I'm going to

37
00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:24.079
type in collision and rather than no collision I'm just going to have a block wall.

38
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.899
Okay so that's just going to control how we do the collisions but there are lots of different

39
00:04:29.899 --> 00:04:34.699
settings here. For example the shadows you might want to play with if you're looking to optimize,

40
00:04:34.699 --> 00:04:39.420
especially for very very small things like the casting of casting shadows here.

41
00:04:42.939 --> 00:04:48.379
That is very costly, can be very costly, so if you've got very small grass or something like

42
00:04:48.939 --> 00:04:55.259
that it might be worth turning off shadows on it in order to get a slightly

43
00:04:56.699 --> 00:05:03.060
to prevent having to pay that massive cost. So we have loads and loads of different settings here

44
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:07.939
all set by the mesh entries. One thing that I'd like to share is something that's called

45
00:05:07.939 --> 00:05:14.339
weights. So you can see here we've got bushes and trees and if we have a look at the world

46
00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:22.110
you can see that we have now a lot of trees. Okay, that's slightly too many. So

47
00:05:23.709 --> 00:05:27.949
I'm going to change around a couple of different things here to reduce the amount of trees.

48
00:05:28.589 --> 00:05:33.230
So the first thing is I'm going to increase the amount of bushes to the trees. So I'll make

49
00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:39.660
two bushes to one tree, force regenerate, still two packed, a little bit less, but we have less

50
00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:49.230
trees here. Now I'm going to go back to the surface sampler and rather than having 1.1,

51
00:05:49.230 --> 00:06:02.379
I'm going to change this to 0.2. That looks a lot more reasonable. There you go,

52
00:06:02.379 --> 00:06:06.459
so we've got fewer trees and a bunch of different bushes all scattered about.

53
00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:16.189
Okay, so that is my starting of my forest. So you should hopefully be able to see something

54
00:06:16.189 --> 00:06:20.509
similar to this. Don't worry, we're going to do a lot more with this, so it'll look a lot more

55
00:06:20.509 --> 00:06:28.269
natural. But we're going to go through the problems sort of one piece, one selection at

56
00:06:28.269 --> 00:06:33.709
a time. I'm going to drag this over here so that I can show what I think is our first

57
00:06:33.709 --> 00:06:39.629
problem. Now you can see if we have a tree, it doesn't matter so much for the bushes,

58
00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:51.120
but we have this tree. When it's creating the static mesh, it is using the ground normal

59
00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:57.600
in order to determine the rotation. And that means we have a bunch of trees which are sticking

60
00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.800
out of the side of the mountain. And trees in real life don't do this. They will grow

61
00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.639
up towards the sun. They might take a little bit into consideration the ground,

62
00:07:07.279 --> 00:07:16.779
but mostly they will ignore it. So that's one problem. The second problem is a lot of these

63
00:07:16.779 --> 00:07:22.699
trees look very similar. And to try and make a natural forest, we want to try and make

64
00:07:22.699 --> 00:07:29.740
everything look as different as possible. But we'd like to reduce the amount of meshes we use,

65
00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:35.100
just because that way we can cut down on the cost of memory used. So we want to use one

66
00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:42.800
mesh, but we want them to look as varied as possible. So what we're going to do is we are

67
00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:52.899
going to jump in and we're going to add in another mesh. And this is when we're talking

68
00:07:52.899 --> 00:08:00.540
about points again. So we've got our points here. And if I press E to disable this,

69
00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:05.259
and if I press D on here so that we can look at what the points look like,

70
00:08:05.980 --> 00:08:13.180
you can see that all of our points are at this moment flat. They're defined by the ground.

71
00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:15.819
So you can see these are the ones that are causing us problems.

72
00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:24.670
And they're all facing in exactly the same direction. So let's change this. What I'm going

73
00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.910
to do is I'm going to jump over here. I'm going to hold down ALT and break this.

74
00:08:28.910 --> 00:08:34.669
Now it's important we break it because I'll demonstrate why. But I want to transform these

75
00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:46.860
points. I'm going to come in here and select a transform points. And now if I plug this in,

76
00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:51.149
what we're going to be actually doing is duplicating these points,

77
00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:58.590
because it's not automatically going to break this. Where if we were working normally with

78
00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:04.590
an execution pin on Blueprints, we would be able to break, because you can only put one

79
00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:10.750
execution pin in some circumstances. But most cases, it would break them.

80
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.269
Wherein this is just going to add them up. And what we're going to end up with is

81
00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.710
a concatenation of the points. Just means it's going to duplicate it.

82
00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:25.470
Okay. So I'm going to hold down ALT to left-click to get rid of that.

83
00:09:28.659 --> 00:09:34.659
So let's have a look at this transform points that we have here. And you can see that we

84
00:09:34.659 --> 00:09:41.620
have a number of different settings. And each of this, we have a offset for the location,

85
00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:48.100
the rotation, and the scale. Now, what we want to do is we want to make these trees,

86
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:53.220
the trees' ambushes look as different as possible. So the first thing I'm going to do

87
00:09:53.220 --> 00:10:01.070
with the offset is to try and avoid the oddness of the roots. I'm going to push it down.

88
00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:09.419
So what I'm going to do is I'm going to come in here and I'm going to put minus 20 and minus 20.

89
00:10:11.539 --> 00:10:16.340
Now, you'll be able to note that there is a minimum offset and a maximum offset.

90
00:10:17.059 --> 00:10:20.899
And this is because by default, it wants to give you a random value.

91
00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:28.659
And so we've always got a maximum and a minimum for it to give you a random value in the range.

92
00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:33.220
We want to make this consistent, so I'm just going to go with minus 20 for both.

93
00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:46.539
Now, rotation is different. What I want to do is I'm going to rotate it by up to 360 degrees

94
00:10:47.539 --> 00:10:55.120
on the Z axis. So this means that it will turn to any different degree. I'm also going to give

95
00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:06.139
it a bit of a random tilt, but not much. Only up to 10% or 10 degrees. There you go.

96
00:11:08.509 --> 00:11:15.870
Now, you'll see under this that we have absolute rotation or absolute offset or absolute scale.

97
00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:23.230
This is because otherwise what it's going to be is it's going to be additive. So in the offset,

98
00:11:23.789 --> 00:11:30.509
I don't want to set the location. I don't want to set the absolute location. I just want to

99
00:11:30.509 --> 00:11:36.669
push it down a little bit. So I'm not going to tick that on. However, for the rotation,

100
00:11:36.669 --> 00:11:42.909
I no longer want it to take the ground into consideration. So I am going to tick on

101
00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:52.000
to have an absolute rotation. Now below this, we have scale. And what I'm going to do

102
00:11:52.000 --> 00:12:00.320
is I want the scale to vary a little tree to tree. So the first thing I'm going to do

103
00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:06.960
is these little padlocks. I'm going to switch these on because I want to keep it as a uniform

104
00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.320
scale. I want to keep these consistent. And the first one I'm going to have as

105
00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:24.450
0.8. And this one I am going to change to 1.2. We could have absolute scale here,

106
00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:29.409
but it doesn't matter because it's defaulting to one. So it doesn't matter very much.

107
00:12:30.129 --> 00:12:34.529
That's going to give us a level of randomness in the points. And because we put a randomness

108
00:12:34.529 --> 00:12:42.049
in the points, that is going to be spread across to the static mesh spawner. I'm just going

109
00:12:42.049 --> 00:12:51.070
to turn off the debug here. Actually, let's look at the debug on the transform points.

110
00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:58.139
So you can now see it's a little bit subtle, but at least you can see that the rotation

111
00:12:58.139 --> 00:13:03.580
is varied. But if we look at the ones that are sitting on here, they're now no longer

112
00:13:03.580 --> 00:13:11.899
conforming to the landscape. They are just standing up straight. We're also pushed a

113
00:13:11.899 --> 00:13:15.019
little bit down, but it's only subtle, so it's hard to see.

114
00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:25.230
Okay. So now let's turn off the debug and turn on the static mesh spawner.

115
00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:35.700
And let's have a look. Okay. And now our forest is looking at a forest. It's more of a wood.

116
00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:41.940
But it's looking a lot more random, at least. Just the rotation of the trees make them look

117
00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:47.139
like they are different trees. And the scaling up and down of the size makes it look a bit more

118
00:13:47.139 --> 00:13:57.870
natural. So we have our trees and some bushes. Thank you for that. So we will stop

119
00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:07.179
there at this point. And next up, we will be adding in some rocks. Okay. See you in the

